// 119 — Overloaded functions — DESIGN NOTE
// C++ / Java let you have `void print(int)` and `void print(string)`
// as two functions with the same name, dispatched by argument type.
// Convenient when you don't want to think about which `print` you
// want; expensive when you do — overload resolution rules in C++
// are notoriously tricky, and a mis-resolved call can pick a
// surprising overload silently.
//
// AiLang resolves names by exact match. There is no per-type
// overloading. The convention instead is to give each operation a
// distinct name that announces its type:
//
//     PrintMessage(s)   // string
//     PrintNumber(n)    // integer
//     PrintChar(c)      // single byte/char
//
// Three names, three call sites that read unambiguously. No
// resolution rules, no quietly-picked-the-wrong-one bugs. This
// composes with the operator-overloading rule (see 056) — same
// philosophy, applied at the function level.
SubRoutine.Main {
    // Three callees, three explicit names — same as if a typed
    // overload had picked them, but readable from the call site alone.
    PrintMessage("text:   ")
    PrintMessage("hello\n")

    PrintMessage("number: ")
    PrintNumber(42)

    PrintMessage("\nchar:   ")
    PrintChar(65)
    PrintMessage("\n")
}
RunTask(Main)
